---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Duel Cut"
  description="A head-to-head movie ranking app that builds a personal canon from choices instead of star ratings."
>
  <article class="kh-case section">
    <header class="kh-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="kh-case-kicker">01 / PRODUCT / FILM / PERSONAL DATA</p>
        <h1 class="kh-case-title">DUEL CUT</h1>
        <p class="kh-case-deck">Your movie taste, directed by choices instead of star ratings.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dl class="kh-case-meta">
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Concept / Product Design / Development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Built</dt>
            <dd>2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Core idea</dt>
            <dd>Pairwise choices become a personal movie ranking.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <div class="kh-case-actions">
          <a class="kh-pill" href="https://iphiginea.github.io/duelcut/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open app ↗</a>
          <a class="kh-text-link" href="https://github.com/iphiginea/duelcut" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="kh-case-gallery kh-case-gallery-duel">
      <img
        src={withBase('/images/duelcut-duel-hd.webp')}
        alt="Duel Cut interface showing a movie duel between Bugonia and Harold and Maude"
        width="600"
        height="959"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="kh-case-body">
      <p class="kh-case-section-label">THE PROJECT</p>
      <div class="kh-case-prose">
        <h2>Why I made it</h2>
        <p>
          Choosing between two movies I love tells me more than giving both of them four stars.
          I wanted a ranking system that behaves more like taste actually does: comparative,
          contextual, and sometimes a little painful.
        </p>

        <h2>What I built</h2>
        <p>
          Duel Cut turns repeated head-to-head movie choices into an evolving personal canon. The
          duel is the input; the ranking is the output. Instead of asking for an abstract score,
          the product keeps presenting a simpler question: which one wins?
        </p>

        <h2>The product logic</h2>
        <p>
          The experience is intentionally narrow. Search and add movies, compare them in duels,
          then watch the ranking change as more decisions accumulate. Rankings and insights give
          the choices a payoff without interrupting the basic loop.
        </p>

        <h2>Design direction</h2>
        <p>
          I treated the interface more like a midnight repertory-cinema program than a generic
          ratings dashboard: dark, poster-led, direct, and built around the tension of the matchup.
          The visual system stays out of the way of the movie art while making each decision feel
          consequential.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="kh-case-next">
      <a href={withBase('/works/encore/')}>
        <span class="kh-case-section-label">NEXT PROJECT</span>
        <strong>Encore →</strong>
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
